# cost_model
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  What happens inside estimate_step_time() step by step
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  One training step = one full forward + backward + gradient update across all GPUs.
   The cost model breaks this into 7 additive terms:

     T_total = T_compute + T_bubble + T_tp_comm + T_pp_comm + T_dp_comm
               + T_step_overhead + T_execution

   Inputs:
     cfg       — model: layers=8, hidden=256, heads=4, seq=64, batch=8
     pp=2, tp=2, dp=2
     profile   — ClusterProfile: α_intra=5µs, β_intra=1ns/B, α_cross=80µs, β_cross=80ns/B,
                   T_block=1ms, T_block_repr=1.9ms
     topology  — TopologyInfo:  tp_intra=True, pp_intra=False, dp_intra=False
     num_microbatches = 4

   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Term 1 — T_compute  (pure GPU compute)
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

   profile.T_block = 1 ms        ← isolated measurement (clean cache)
   profile.T_block_repr = 1.9 ms ← representative measurement (with M=4 microbatch
                                    activations resident in memory)

   Conditional selection:
     If pp = 1: use T_block (isolated) — no pipeline, no simultaneous microbatches
     If pp > 1: use T_block_repr — 1F1B keeps M microbatches in-flight

   With tensor parallelism (tp=2), each GPU handles only half the weight
   matrices per block, so one block takes T_block_repr / tp = 0.95 ms.

   With pipeline parallelism (pp=2), each rank holds layers / pp = 4 blocks.

   With num_microbatches=4, each rank runs 4 microbatches sequentially:

     T_compute = layers_per_stage × (T_block_repr / tp) × num_microbatches
               = 4 × (1.9ms / 2) × 4
               = 15.2 ms

   Why the conditional matters:
     Isolated T_block (1 ms) would give 8 ms — a 2× underestimate.
     The representative T_block_repr absorbs memory pressure, L2 cache pollution,
     and allocator fragmentation that exist during real 1F1B execution.
     (Empirically: 1.90 ms isolated → 3.68 ms with M=8 activations on this cluster.)

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Term 2 — T_bubble  (1F1B pipeline schedule overhead)
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Pipeline parallelism with pp stages uses the 1F1B (one-forward-one-backward)
  schedule.  At the very start and end of each step, some stages sit idle
  waiting for the pipeline to fill or drain.

  The number of wasted slots = pp - 1 = 1  (one full microbatch duration)
  Total slots = num_microbatches = 4

  Bubble fraction = (pp - 1) / num_microbatches = 1 / 4 = 25%

    T_bubble = bubble_fraction × T_compute
             = 0.25 × 8 ms
             = 2 ms

  Visualised for pp=2, num_microbatches=4:

  Stage 0:  F0 F1 F2 F3  B3 B2 B1 B0
                                       ↑ waiting here = 1 bubble slot
  Stage 1:  -- F0 F1 F2  F3 B3 B2 B1  B0
              ↑ idle

  The bubble fraction (pp-1)/M shrinks as you use more microbatches.
  Using M=8 instead of M=4 would halve T_bubble.

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Term 3 — T_tp_comm  (TP AllReduce per layer)
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Tensor parallelism (Megatron-LM column-row style) inserts 2 AllReduce calls
  per transformer block in the **forward pass**:
    - one after the attention output projection (row linear)
    - one after the MLP second linear (row linear)

  NOTE: The backward pass also has 2 additional AllReduces for the column-
  parallel layers (c_attn, mlp.c_fc), but ColossalAI launches them
  asynchronously (async_op=True) so they overlap with the weight-gradient
  matmul. Their exposed latency on the critical path is effectively zero,
  so the cost model counts only the forward-exposed 2 AllReduces.

  The tensor being all-reduced is the activation: (microbatch, seq, hidden)
    microbatch_size = batch / num_microbatches = 8 / 4 = 2
    act_bytes = 2 × 64 × 256 × 2  =  65,536 bytes  =  64 KB

  Ring AllReduce cost for n=tp=2 participants, S=64 KB:
    T = 2 × (n-1)/n × (α + β × S)
      = 2 × (1/2) × (5µs + 1ns/B × 65536 B)
      = 1 × (5µs + 65.5µs)
      = 70.5 µs  per AllReduce

  topology.tp_intra_node = True  →  use α_intra, β_intra  (fast PCIe link)

  Per layer = 2 AllReduces × 70.5 µs = 141 µs
  Total over all layers and all microbatches:
    T_tp_comm = layers_per_stage × 2 × T_allreduce × num_microbatches
              = 4 × 2 × 70.5µs × 4
              = 2.26 ms

  If tp were cross-node (tp=4, spanning all 8 ranks across 3 nodes):
    T = 2 × (3/4) × (80µs + 80ns/B × 65536 B)
      = 1.5 × (80µs + 5.24ms)
      = 7.98 ms per AllReduce
    T_tp_comm = 4 × 2 × 7.98ms × 4 = 255 ms  — 100× worse!
  This is why the pruning rule in search.py rejects tp > min(node_gpus).

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Term 4 — T_pp_comm  (PP P2P send/recv at stage boundaries)
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  At each pipeline stage boundary one rank sends its activation output to
  the next stage via dist.send / dist.recv.

  The activation tensor is the same size as the TP AllReduce tensor: 64 KB.

  P2P send cost = α + β × S  (point-to-point, not ring)

  topology.pp_intra_node = False  →  use α_cross, β_cross  (100 GbE)
    T_p2p = 80µs + 80ns/B × 65536 B
           = 80µs + 5,242µs
           = 5,322 µs  ≈  5.3 ms per microbatch boundary

  In a 1F1B schedule the send is pipelined with compute (the next microbatch's
  forward starts while the send is in flight).  We count it conservatively
  as a serialised cost multiplied by num_microbatches:

    T_pp_comm = num_microbatches × T_p2p
              = 4 × 5.3 ms
              = 21.3 ms

  Note: this is the dominant non-compute cost for this plan precisely because
  the activation tensor is large (64 KB) and the Ethernet β is high (80 ns/B).
  A smaller hidden dimension or shorter sequence would reduce this significantly.

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Term 5 — T_dp_comm  (DP gradient AllReduce, partially overlapped)
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  After the backward pass all dp=2 peers must synchronise their gradients.

  Gradient size per GPU:
    One transformer block has roughly 12 × H² parameters (attention Q/K/V/out
    + MLP fc1/fc2 + two LayerNorm).  With tp=2 each GPU holds only 1/tp of
    each layer's parameters.

    param_bytes = (3H² + H² + 4H² + 4H²) × dtype_bytes
                = 12 × 256² × 2  =  1,572,864 bytes  ≈  1.5 MB  per full layer

    Per GPU per layer = 1.5 MB / tp = 0.75 MB
    layers_per_stage = 4
    total_grad_bytes = 4 × 0.75 MB = 3 MB

  Ring AllReduce for n=dp=2 participants, S=3 MB:
    T_raw = 2 × (1/2) × (80µs + 80ns/B × 3,145,728 B)
           = 1 × (80µs + 251,658µs)
           = 251,738 µs  ≈  252 ms

  topology.dp_intra_node = False  →  use α_cross, β_cross  (Ethernet)

  DDP/ZeRO buckets and overlaps gradient communication with the backward pass.
  Empirically ~70% is hidden.  We apply an overlap_factor = 0.3:

     T_dp_comm = 0.3 × 252 ms = 75.6 ms

   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Term 6 — T_step_overhead  (embedding + LM head + loss, once per step)
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

   The profiler measures an isolated transformer block, but a real step also
   includes token embedding, LM head projection, and cross-entropy loss.
   These run once per step (not per microbatch).

   Scaled proportionally to T_block:
     T_embedding ≈ T_block × (Vocab × Hidden) / (12 × H²) × 0.5  ≈ 0.5 ms
     T_lm_head   ≈ T_block × FLOP_ratio × 3                        ≈ 2.0 ms
     T_loss      ≈ T_block × max(FLOP_ratio, 0.005)                ≈ 0.1 ms

     T_step_overhead ≈ 2.6 ms

   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Term 7 — T_execution  (framework overhead: AdamW + NCCL + dispatch)
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

   Four physically based components with GPU-specific coefficients:

   1. AdamW optimizer step:
        T_adam = 4 × local_params / BW_adam  ≈ 4 × 0.75MB / 126GB/s  ≈ 0.02 ms

   2. NCCL launch overhead:
        100 µs per collective × (2×layers_per_stage×M TP collectives + 1 DP)
        ≈ 100µs × (2×4×4 + 1) ≈ 3.3 ms

   3. PP stage transitions:
        M × (pp-1) × 0.5 ms = 4 × 1 × 0.5 ms = 2.0 ms

   4. Python dispatch per block:
        M × layers_per_stage × (0.15 + 0.05×(tp-1)) ms
        = 4 × 4 × 0.20 ms = 3.2 ms

     T_execution ≈ 8.5 ms

   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Final total for (pp=2, tp=2, dp=2)
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

   T_total    = 124.3 ms
     compute      =  15.2 ms  (12.2%)
     bubble       =   2.0 ms  ( 1.6%)
     TP comm      =   2.3 ms  ( 1.8%)
     PP comm      =  21.3 ms  (17.1%)
     DP comm      =  75.6 ms  (60.8%)
     step OH      =   2.6 ms  ( 2.1%)
     execution OH =   8.5 ms  ( 6.8%)

   The DP AllReduce still dominates (~61%), but execution overhead is now
   visible (~7%). The representative T_block increased compute from 8 ms
   to 15 ms, correcting the previous systematic underestimate.

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Comparing all candidates for the [2,4,2] cluster
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  (Same profile and model config as above.)

  Plan              T_total    compute   bubble   TP_comm  PP_comm  DP_comm  pp_intra  dp_intra
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  pp=1 tp=1 dp=8    561.2 ms    32.0      0.0      0.0      0.0    529.2      True     False
  pp=1 tp=2 dp=4    247.3 ms    16.0      0.0      4.5      0.0    226.8      True     False
  pp=2 tp=1 dp=4    247.1 ms    16.0      4.0      0.0      0.3    226.8      True     False  ← PP now intra!
  pp=2 tp=2 dp=2    109.2 ms     8.0      2.0      2.3     21.3     75.6     False     False
  pp=4 tp=1 dp=2    110.9 ms     8.0      6.0      0.0     21.3     75.6     False     False  ← DP now cross!
  pp=4 tp=2 dp=1  *  29.4 ms     4.0      3.0      1.1     21.3      0.0     False      True
  pp=8 tp=1 dp=1     32.3 ms     4.0      7.0      0.0     21.3      0.0     False      True

  (dp_outside=True — HybridParallelPlugin default, mesh shape = (dp, pp, tp))

  Observations:
    1. Plans with large dp over Ethernet (dp=4, dp=8) pay a huge gradient sync
       cost — DP AllReduce dominates everything else.
    2. pp=2 tp=1 dp=4: PP is now intra-node (0.3 ms) because each DP replica's
       two stages land on the same physical node with dp_outside=True. But DP
       AllReduce at 226 ms still dominates — it still scores poorly overall.
    3. pp=4 tp=1 dp=2: DP is cross-node (75 ms), making it 110 ms total —
       comparable to pp=2 tp=2 dp=2 and far worse than the winner.
    4. pp=4 tp=2 dp=1 scores best (29.4 ms): dp=1 eliminates DP AllReduce
       entirely, TP stays intra-node (1.1 ms), and the unavoidable cross-node
       PP (21.3 ms) is the only significant communication cost.

  Note: search.py will also apply memory pruning (model shard must fit on one
  GPU) which may eliminate some plans not shown here.

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Key formula reference
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Ring AllReduce (n participants, S bytes, link α/β):
    T = 2 × (n-1)/n × (α + β × S)

  Point-to-point P2P send (S bytes, link α/β):
    T = α + β × S

  Activation tensor size (microbatch b, seq L, hidden H, dtype d bytes):
    S_act = b × L × H × d

  Parameter bytes per layer (hidden H, dtype d bytes):
    S_param ≈ (3H² + H² + 4H² + 4H²) × d = 12H² × d

   T_compute  = (layers/pp) × (T_block^eff/tp) × M
     where T_block^eff = T_block       if pp = 1 (isolated)
                         = T_block_repr if pp > 1 (representative, with M activations)
   T_bubble   = (pp-1)/(M+pp-1) × T_compute   (pp > 1)
   T_tp_comm  = (layers/pp) × 2 × AllReduce(S_act, tp, tp_link) × M
   T_pp_comm  = M × P2P(S_act, pp_link)       (pp > 1)
   T_dp_comm  = overlap_factor × AllReduce(S_param × (layers/pp) / tp, dp, dp_link)
   T_step     = T_embedding + T_lm_head + T_loss
   T_exec     = T_adam + T_nccl + T_pp_trans + T_dispatch

   where M = num_microbatches, link = intra if group is same-node else cross.
   overlap_factor depends on T_compute vs T_allreduce_raw and ddp_efficiency.

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Unit test (no cluster needed)
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  from colossalai.auto_parallel.hybrid_planner.cost_model import ModelConfig, estimate_step_time
  from colossalai.auto_parallel.hybrid_planner.profiler import ClusterProfile
  from colossalai.auto_parallel.hybrid_planner.topology import classify_comms

  profile = ClusterProfile(
      alpha_intra=5e-6, beta_intra=1e-9,
      alpha_cross=80e-6, beta_cross=80e-9,
      T_block=1e-3,
  )
  cfg = ModelConfig(layers=8, hidden=256, heads=4, seq=64, batch=8, dtype_bytes=2)

  topo = classify_comms([2, 4, 2], pp=2, tp=2, dp=2)
  bd   = estimate_step_time(cfg, pp=2, tp=2, dp=2, profile=profile,
                            topology=topo, num_microbatches=4)
  print(bd)
  # T_total = ~109 ms

